  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1002 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nZonal flow across the area tonight as original trough well to our \neast, as next Pacific low/trough moves onshore. Ahead of this a \nshort-wave upper ridge currently is moving across New Mexico in the \nzonal flow. Deeper sub-tropical moisture persists just south of New \nMexico. Mid-level flow somewhat influenced by Rachel remains \nsoutherly, help keeping this moisture in place. Elsewhere, \nconsiderable very high level moisture over much of New Mexico but \nhasn't resulted in much of any cirrus.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...Pacific trough moves over the Great \nBasin/Arizona Tuesday. Persistent dewpoints in the upper 40s will \nlikely lead to some more CU fields Monday and maybe some higher \nclouds Tuesday as the trough approaches. Temperatures warming back \nto near normal by Tuesday. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...trough moves over New Mexico Wednesday and \nthen drops south over west Texas Thursday. PWs increase back to .70 \ninches north to .80 to 1.00 inch south. Airmass becomes moderately \nunstable so we should see a slight chance of showers or \nthunderstorms both days. Temperatures remaining seasonable.\n\nFriday into the weekend...low finally ejects out to our east by \nSaturday. At upper levels weak short-wave begins bringing moisture \nin Saturday night and Sunday, while at mid levels south/southwest \nflow begins bringing in some sub-tropical moisture and PWs approach \nan inch in the south Sunday. So another slight chance of showers or \na thunderstorms Sunday.\n\nNote: ECMWF still tries bringing the remnants of what would be Simon \nacross west Texas Monday night/Tuesday. The various ECMWF runs have \nbeen intermittently showing this; however the ECMWF ensembles do not \nshow this, more in line with the various GFS runs. Worth watching \nthough.",
